  <div class="post-meta"><span>2026</span><span class="blog-tag">IRS News</span><span>Guzman Tax Accounting</span></div>
  <div class="post-featured-img"><img src="/assets/contabilidad-para-negocios-en-usa.webp" alt="Business Accounting in the United States" loading="lazy"/></div>
  <div class="post-content"><p>Running a business in the United States as a Latino comes with its own challenges — and one of the biggest is understanding how American accounting works. The rules are different from those in our home countries, and not knowing them can bring serious consequences with the IRS.</p><h2>What is business accounting in the USA?</h2><p>Business accounting in the USA is the system of recording, organizing, and reporting all of your company's financial transactions according to IRS rules and American accounting standards. It ranges from the daily recording of income and expenses to the preparation of annual tax returns.</p><h2>What does the IRS require from every business in the USA?</h2><p>The IRS requires every business to keep clear, organized financial records. Specifically, you need:</p><ul><li>A record of all income received</li><li>A record of all business expenses</li><li>Supporting documents such as invoices and receipts</li><li>Tax returns filed on time</li><li>Payroll records if you have employees</li></ul><p>Failing to meet these requirements can result in automatic penalties, audits, or legal problems.</p><h2>What type of business do you have, and how does it affect your accounting?</h2><p>In the USA, your business's legal entity type determines how you file taxes:</p><ul><li>Sole Proprietor — you file on your personal return using Schedule C.</li><li>Single-member LLC — treated the same as a sole proprietor by default, but with more legal protection.</li><li>S-Corp or Corporation — a completely separate corporate return with more complex rules.</li><li>Multi-member LLC — requires a separate return as a partnership.</li></ul><p>Choosing the wrong structure can cost you more in taxes than necessary every year.</p><h2>The most common accounting mistakes among Latino businesses in the USA</h2><p>Mixing personal and business accounts is mistake number one. The IRS sees this as a red flag. Other common mistakes include not keeping receipts, not reporting cash income, and not making quarterly estimated payments when required.</p><h2>How often do you need to report to the IRS?</h2><p>Depending on your business, it can be quarterly or annual. Businesses that expect to owe more than $1,000 in taxes for the year must make quarterly estimated payments. Failing to do so triggers automatic penalties.</p><h2>Why you need an accountant who understands your business</h2><p>American accounting has specific rules that change every year. An accountant experienced with Latino businesses in the USA doesn't just handle your numbers — they protect you, save you money, and keep you compliant with the IRS all year long.</p><h2>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we explain everything in Spanish</h2><p>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we work with Latino business owners in Las Vegas who want to understand their finances and grow without fear of the IRS. We handle your complete accounting — bookkeeping, taxes, payroll, and more. Call us at (725) 340-6758 or reach out on WhatsApp.</p><a href="/en/consultation" class="btn btn-primary" style="margin-top:8px;">Get a Consultation</a></div>
